/*! @arch_page arch-disagg-step-down-concurrency Asynchronous step-down concurrency model

# Summary: cursor operations and transactions

To understand synchronization during asynchronous step-down, it is important to first understand
what it affects. Asynchronous step-down affects two decisions made by each cursor operation:

- Whether to access the stable table, the ingest table, or both, and which stable table view to use.
- Whether to roll back the transaction. Transaction commit can also make this decision.

These are the situations in which a transaction is rolled back:

- A write transaction straddles the setting of a step-down timestamp.
- A read transaction without a read timestamp spans the step-down reconfiguration.

Schema operations also change their behavior after the step-down timestamp is set.

Setting the step-down timestamp can run concurrently with cursor and schema operations, so these
decisions require synchronization.

# Terminology and scope

This model explains synchronization during asynchronous step-down; see @ref disagg-roles-step-down
for the transition overview and application requirements. It considers one leader-to-follower
transition rather than multiple subsequent role transitions, because transactions must finish
before the step-up reconfiguration. The following terms are used throughout the document.

- The **global role** is connection-wide and identifies whether the node operates
  as leader or follower. The leader uses a **live B-tree view** of the stable table; the follower
  uses a **read-only checkpoint view**.
- The **begin-time step-down timestamp state** records whether a transaction observed the
  step-down timestamp as set at transaction begin.
- A **step-down-created table** is created while the step-down timestamp is set and initially has
  only an ingest table.
- The **step-down-created flag** records that a layered table has no stable table because it was
  created while the step-down timestamp was set.
- The **role generation** is a version number recorded for a snapshot and advanced during a role
  change.
- The **step-down schema epoch** marks the corresponding boundary for schema operations when
  schema epochs are used.

@anchor disagg-step-down-reconfiguration-locks
# Ordering during the step-down reconfiguration

The step-down reconfiguration is where most changes requiring different ordering guarantees
occur. It changes the stable table view, publishes the global role as follower, and only then
clears the step-down timestamp.

Before publishing the follower role, the step-down reconfiguration marks each open disaggregated
B-tree read-only and its data handle outdated. It holds the data handle list read lock,
which keeps data handles from being removed from the list during the marking walk. It also obtains
exclusive eviction access for each tree while marking it (see
@ref disagg-step-down-eviction "eviction synchronization" for more details).

The checkpoint and schema locks cover the entire step-down reconfiguration. The checkpoint lock
makes the step-down reconfiguration wait for a checkpoint in progress and prevents a checkpoint
from reconciling the B-trees being marked read-only. It also keeps the latest checkpoint timestamp
unchanged while it is verified. When both locks are needed, the checkpoint lock must be taken
before the schema lock to avoid deadlocks. The schema lock orders the step-down reconfiguration
against schema operations and fresh opens of a live B-tree view, which also
decides the @ref disagg-step-down-cursor-open-races "cursor-open race".

The step-down write lock covers clearing the step-down timestamp and step-down schema epoch.
It makes observing the cleared step-down timestamp at transaction begin happen after publishing
the global role as follower. Later cursor operations of a transaction that observes the timestamp
as cleared are therefore guaranteed to observe the global role as follower.

The role generation is advanced immediately before publishing the global role as
follower.

To provide the full picture, the main lock ordering is:

```
The step-down reconfiguration {
    acquire the checkpoint lock
    acquire the schema lock
    acquire the data handle list read lock

    for each data handle except the shared history store {
        if the handle is for a layered table: clear the step-down-created flag
        if the handle is for an open disaggregated B-tree that is not already read-only {
            obtain exclusive eviction access
            mark the B-tree read-only and its data handle outdated
            release exclusive eviction access
        }
    }
    with exclusive eviction access, mark the shared history store B-tree read-only
    and its data handle outdated
    advance the role generation
    publish the global role as follower with a release store
    release the data handle list read lock

    acquire the step-down write lock
    clear the step-down timestamp and step-down schema epoch
    release the step-down write lock

    release the schema lock
    release the checkpoint lock
}
```

@anchor disagg-step-down-schema-operations
# Schema operations

Creating a layered table while the step-down timestamp is set creates only its local ingest table.
Schema creation, where this choice is made, and schema opening, verification, and
deletion all hold the global schema lock. Setting the step-down timestamp takes the same lock, so
these operations are ordered relative to one another.

Opening a layered table while observing the step-down timestamp as set and finding no stable table
sets the step-down-created flag on its data handle. Cursors use the flag to avoid opening the
absent stable table. The step-down reconfiguration clears the flag before publishing the global
role as follower.

The step-down schema epoch is also cleared during the step-down reconfiguration.

# Transaction access to the stable and ingest tables

## Write transaction routing

A cursor write operation must decide which table or tables will receive its update. That decision
uses the following state:

- The begin-time step-down timestamp state.
- The global role observed by the cursor operation.
- The step-down-created flag on the table's data handle.
- Whether write mirroring is enabled.

| Global role | Step-down-created | Set at transaction begin | Write mirroring | Write target |
| ----------- | ----------------- | ------------------------ | --------------- | ------------ |
| Follower | Either | Either | Either | Ingest table only |
| Leader | Yes | Either | Either | Ingest table only |
| Leader | No | No | Either | Stable table only |
| Leader | No | Yes | Enabled | Stable and ingest tables |
| Leader | No | Yes | Disabled | Ingest table only |

The write-mirroring setting is connection-wide and does not change during cursor operations, so it
needs no additional synchronization.

Write transactions do not run during the step-down reconfiguration. The application must stop
serving writes before it and may resume them afterwards, so write operations do not race with
publishing the global role as follower.

Setting the step-down timestamp and observing it at transaction begin use the same step-down
read-write lock. A write transaction that observes it as set can rely on it remaining set until the
transaction ends: the application must finish all write transactions before the step-down
reconfiguration. Cursor operations can always rely on the begin-time step-down timestamp state
because only explicit transactions are allowed for disaggregated connections.

The same requirement protects write operations from racing with clearing the step-down-created
flag. A write transaction that observes the flag as set can rely on it remaining set until the
transaction ends. After the step-down reconfiguration, a write operation observes the global
role as follower and the flag as cleared.

Before the step-down reconfiguration, any access to the stable table uses the live B-tree view.
Afterwards, it uses the read-only checkpoint view. Write transactions need no additional
synchronization for this switch because none run during the step-down reconfiguration.

@anchor disagg-step-down-read-routing
## Read transaction routing

Read transactions on disaggregated connections are always started explicitly with snapshot
isolation. This means that each transaction acquires a snapshot at transaction begin and continues
to read that state without incorporating later changes, which is important for concurrency
management.

Since both observing the step-down timestamp at transaction begin and setting it acquire the same
lock, read transactions can be separated into those whose timestamp observation precedes setting
it and those whose observation follows it. Another separation is between transactions that
acquired their snapshot before the step-down reconfiguration and those that acquired it afterwards.

During the step-down reconfiguration, WiredTiger first publishes the global role as follower and
only then clears the step-down timestamp under the write lock. This guarantees that a transaction
which observes the timestamp as cleared at transaction begin also observes the global role as
follower in its later cursor operations.

### Read transactions beginning "before" the step-down timestamp was set

When observing the step-down timestamp at transaction begin precedes setting it, these
transactions record that it was not set and have already acquired their snapshot on the leader.
They can continue reading only the stable table throughout asynchronous step-down until they
observe the effects of the step-down reconfiguration. Consulting the ingest table would not change
their reads, because ingest table writes made after setting the step-down timestamp are not visible
to that earlier snapshot.

Once these transactions span the step-down reconfiguration, their stable table reads must switch
to the read-only checkpoint view. Cursor operations do not take a lock that prevents the global
role from changing while they observe it, so not every operation is guaranteed to observe the
follower role immediately. An operation may already be in progress or have passed the point where
it observes the role. However, this is safe because the step-down reconfiguration marks the stable
B-trees read-only, and the @ref disagg-step-down-eviction "eviction synchronization" protects
the old stable table while readers still use it. This allows a cursor to reopen the checkpoint view
later.

Moreover, only read transactions with a read timestamp can safely reopen the stable table in this
view, because the history store protects their reads. Read transactions that span the step-down
reconfiguration without a read timestamp are rolled back, as described in the
@ref disagg-step-down-read-rollback "reader rollback section".

### Read transactions beginning with the step-down timestamp set

These transactions observe the step-down timestamp as set at transaction begin. To continue reading
across the step-down reconfiguration, they must also consult the ingest table, alongside the stable
table when it exists, because not all the changes they need are included in the concluding
checkpoint. Changes committed with timestamps above the step-down timestamp are available only
through the ingest table after the step-down reconfiguration, when reads switch to the stable
table's read-only checkpoint view.

To keep the acquired snapshot consistent with the begin-time step-down timestamp state,
transaction begin first acquires a snapshot and then, under the step-down read lock, observes
whether the step-down timestamp is set. In this case, a cursor cannot observe the global role as
leader and use a begin-time step-down timestamp state of not set while its snapshot is new enough
to read ingest table writes made after setting the step-down timestamp.

These transactions can access a table created after the step-down timestamp was set, provided
its creation preceded transaction begin. Such a table has only an ingest table, as described in
@ref disagg-step-down-schema-operations "the schema operations section". Opening such a table on
the leader while the step-down timestamp is set initializes the step-down-created flag before
cursor operations can use its data handle. The handle-opening synchronization makes that initial
state visible to the operations. While the flag remains set, those operations skip opening the
absent stable table.

The global role and the step-down-created flag must agree once a cursor observes the global role
as follower. Cursor operations therefore observe the role with an acquire load before observing
the flag, and the step-down reconfiguration uses a release store to publish the follower role
only after clearing the flag, so an operation that observes the global role as follower must also
observe the flag as cleared.

@anchor disagg-step-down-table-open-race
However, this flag is cleared during the step-down reconfiguration before the global role is
published as follower. Cursor operations do not take the schema lock just to observe the flag,
so an operation racing with clearing it may observe the flag as cleared while still using an
earlier observation of the global role as leader. In this case, the cursor tries to open the
stable table. Both that fresh open and the step-down reconfiguration take the schema lock, so
the open must wait for the step-down reconfiguration to finish and is then guaranteed to observe
the global role as follower. The missing stable table is tolerated in this case. Later cursor
operations are also guaranteed to observe the global role as follower.

### Read transactions beginning in the follower role

Read transactions whose snapshots are established while observing the global role as follower
can read the follower view and need no additional step-down-specific synchronization. If snapshot
acquisition observes the global role as follower and validates the recorded role generation,
the transaction's cursor operations are also guaranteed to observe the global role as follower.

A transaction can observe the step-down timestamp as cleared at transaction begin while its
snapshot still records the global role as leader. Cursor operations choose which tables to open
using a separate observation of the global role for each operation, not the snapshot's recorded
role. Publishing the follower role is ordered before observing the cleared timestamp under the
step-down lock, so these later role observations are guaranteed to find the global role as
follower. The @ref disagg-step-down-read-rollback "reader rollback rules" still apply to these
transactions.

@anchor disagg-step-down-cursor-open-races
## Cursor opens racing with the step-down reconfiguration

Cursor operations that race with the step-down reconfiguration need separate consideration.

During the step-down reconfiguration, stable B-trees are marked read-only and their data handles
outdated before the global role is published as follower. A cursor operation can therefore
observe the stable table's data handle as outdated while the global role recorded for that
operation is still leader. The ingest table remains accessible throughout the transition, but
a fresh open of the stable table's live B-tree view must not succeed once the node is a follower.

This case is handled because a fresh open of the stable table's live B-tree view takes the schema
lock, as does the step-down reconfiguration. Once the stable table's data handle is marked
outdated, the session and connection handle lookups skip it, so any later open of the live B-tree
view is a fresh open. If the step-down reconfiguration acquires the lock
first, the open waits and then observes the updated global role as follower. The cursor operation
returns `WT_ROLLBACK` rather than opening the live B-tree view. If the application retries the
transaction, it observes the global role as follower.

If the stable table's live B-tree view opens before the step-down reconfiguration, its B-tree is
included in the read-only marking walk so the cursors can continue accessing it for the time being.

## Rolling back transactions that span step-down boundaries

@anchor disagg-step-down-write-rollback
### Write transactions spanning setting the step-down timestamp

The check that guarantees rolling back a write transaction spanning setting the step-down
timestamp is at transaction commit. Transaction begin and the commit-time check observe whether
the step-down timestamp is set under the same read lock, while setting the timestamp takes the
write lock. A write transaction is rolled back if the timestamp was not set when it began but is
set at the commit-time check. Cursor write operations can also roll back such a transaction early,
but these checks are purely a performance optimization: they do not acquire the lock and do not
establish the happens-before edge that guarantees detection at commit.

The lock is released before the rest of transaction commit, so the check, not commit completion,
determines whether the transaction spans setting the step-down timestamp. A transaction that
passes the check can therefore finish committing after the step-down timestamp is set. This is
safe provided the application guarantees that this transaction's commit timestamp for writes to
the stable table is at or below the step-down timestamp.

@anchor disagg-step-down-read-rollback
### Read transactions without a read timestamp spanning the step-down reconfiguration

Read transactions without a read timestamp that span the step-down reconfiguration must be rolled
back. Their snapshot was acquired against the live B-tree view and cannot safely describe the
read-only checkpoint view. The differences in transaction identifier representation behind this
are outside the scope of this document.

To detect such transactions, snapshot acquisition at transaction begin enters the current role
generation and records the global role in the session. The step-down reconfiguration advances
the generation before publishing the global role as follower. A cursor operation that opens or
reopens the stable table compares the global role it observes and the current role generation
with those recorded for the snapshot. If the transaction has no read timestamp and either
differs, the operation returns `WT_ROLLBACK`. See @ref generations for generation semantics and
the lifetime guarantees they provide.

Comparing the role generation can also detect a step-down reconfiguration that advanced the
generation before publishing the follower role. The global role is published as follower with
a release store after that advance, so a cursor that observes it as follower with an acquire load
must also observe the advanced role generation.

A transaction that races with the step-down reconfiguration while acquiring its snapshot has
not yet read under that snapshot. Snapshot acquisition checks the recorded global role and role
generation after the scan and rebuilds the snapshot if either changed, rather than rolling back
the transaction.

Read transactions with a read timestamp do not need the role-and-generation check when opening
or reopening the stable table because the history store protects their reads. Their read timestamp
must still be at least the latest published checkpoint's oldest timestamp, which is no older than
that of the checkpoint being opened.

@anchor disagg-step-down-eviction
## Eviction synchronization

Setting a B-tree's read-only flag does not by itself stop eviction already in progress from writing
shared pages. The step-down reconfiguration therefore obtains exclusive eviction access for
each open disaggregated B-tree that is not already read-only, marks the tree read-only and its
data handle outdated, then re-enables eviction. The ingest table is not marked. The shared history
store is processed last so eviction can still use it while draining the other trees.

Exclusive eviction access prevents new normal eviction for the tree, interrupts and clears its
walk, removes queued candidates, and waits for normal page eviction already in progress to finish.
Eviction remains disabled until the tree is marked read-only and its data handle outdated.

Readers may still be using the old stable B-tree after it is marked. Eviction must retain any page
whose content cannot be read back from shared storage while the old data handle is still in use.
Once the last reader releases that handle, eviction can discard the remaining pages without
reconciling them to shared storage.

## Detecting write conflicts during asynchronous step-down

### Why writes to the ingest table need a cross-table check

The @ref disagg-roles-step-down overview describes write routing. Without write mirroring,
transactions writing to different tables do not share an update chain, so a single-tree conflict
check cannot detect a conflicting update in the other table.

A write to the ingest table therefore checks the key in both tables when the transaction observed
the step-down timestamp as set at transaction begin.

Mirrored writes do not need this cross-table check because writing to the stable table already
uses the stable table's single-tree conflict check.

@anchor disagg-step-down-table-conflicts
### Transactions writing to the stable table versus transactions writing to the ingest table

Consider a transaction that passes its commit-time check before the step-down timestamp is set,
and another that observes the timestamp as set at transaction begin. The first writes to the
stable table and can commit; the second writes to the ingest table. Their writes are ordered as
follows:

1. The transaction writing to the stable table publishes its writes before its read-locked
   commit-time check.
2. Setting the step-down timestamp acquires the write lock after that check releases the read lock.
3. The transaction writing to the ingest table takes the read lock after the step-down timestamp
   is set and records observing it as set.
4. Its cursor operations run after that begin-time observation.

The write lock taken when setting the step-down timestamp connects the two read-locked sections;
read locks alone would not order them. The transaction writing to the ingest table must therefore
observe the stable table's published updates when checking the update chains. However, passing
the check for the transaction writing to the stable table does not mean its commit has completed.
The transaction writing to the ingest table acquires its snapshot before observing the step-down
timestamp under the lock, so that snapshot may still exclude the transaction writing to the
stable table, even if it has committed by the time the locked timestamp observation finishes.
The ingest table write must therefore check the stable table for a conflict: an ordinary lookup
can miss an update excluded by the snapshot, and the ingest table's own conflict check cannot
inspect that update in the stable table.

### Conflicts with write transactions spanning setting the step-down timestamp

A write transaction that spans setting the step-down timestamp observes it as not set at
transaction begin and reaches its commit-time check after it is set. Its cursor write checks can
miss the set timestamp because they do not acquire the step-down lock, so it may still write to
the stable table while another transaction writes to the ingest table. No lock ordering guarantees
that either transaction observes the other's writes.

Both transactions may temporarily write the same logical key into different tables, but the locked
commit-time check must roll back the transaction that spans setting the step-down timestamp, so
they cannot both commit. Other snapshot readers cannot read its uncommitted writes, and rollback
marks them aborted. If the transaction writing to the ingest table observes one of these updates,
its conflict check may roll it back too; this is safe even though the other transaction must also
roll back. Transactions writing to the same table continue to use its single-tree conflict check.

*/
